WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.800
Modal dialogs, as we said, cut the user off from the content on the page, so they can't interact with it,

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:14.920
so they can be annoying at times. So we need to see when to use them, so they fit and don't annoy the user.

3
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:24.660
The first case where a modal dialog isn't annoying: when there's a warning, and I want to alert the user right now to something they need to pay attention to,

4
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:34.520
or take a certain action, or notice that what they're doing will lead to something else they haven't noticed, to prevent an error.

5
00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:44.240
Here a modal dialog fits, because I'm alerting the user to something important. So fine, I'll make them focus on the warning, and they can't interact with the content behind it.

6
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.240
Here, the use is right.

7
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:56.500
A second case for modal dialogs: when I need data or information from the user that they must enter to finish the process they're in right now.

8
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:08.700
Say the user is booking something on my website, or ordering a product, and I need them to sign in or sign up so they can order it or finish the booking,

9
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:22.760
then a modal dialog can appear and cut them off from the content behind it. They haven't left the booking or purchase they were in at all,

10
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:36.180
a modal dialog just appeared in the same context, cut them off from the background, had them sign in or sign up, and then they go back to what they were doing.

11
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:48.120
So I got both benefits: I didn't take them out of their context, and I had them focus on another process they need to continue, and when they're done they go back to it.

12
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.460
That's one of the main strengths of dialogs, and modal dialogs in particular.

13
00:01:52.460 --> 00:02:03.620
Another use of modal dialogs: when I want the user to go through a somewhat complex process with several steps, all following each other inside the same modal dialog.

14
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:15.560
I can also use a modal dialog to ask the user for data at this moment that will make later steps easier and cut the effort they'd spend.

15
00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:31.080
The user can close the modal dialog and ignore it, or give me the information because it makes things easier for them, and it also helps me improve their experience. Then I can use a modal dialog too.

16
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:41.720
But I certainly won't use a modal dialog for information I can get later in another way, or information that isn't that important in the first place.

17
00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:55.840
Instead, the user can see a message asking if they can give me this data now, and if they agree, a modal dialog or a page opens where they enter it.

18
00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:05.860
So I won't use one when I can get this information later, or it isn't important, or it won't help in the flow the user is going through or in their main steps.

19
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:15.960
And as we said, a modal dialog cuts the user off from the data they're working with or the page content, and makes them focus on the process or dialog in front of them.

20
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:26.340
So if the user is in an important process like checkout, they're checking out, it's not a good idea at all to show them a modal dialog while they focus on it.

21
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:39.660
Most users feel a little anxious in this process; they need to do it carefully and they're focused on it. A modal dialog would be very annoying, so here I won't use one at all.

22
00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:49.160
I also won't use a modal dialog when the user has to make a decision based on other data, information they still need to see somewhere else on the website.

23
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:57.120
Then I can use a non-modal dialog, or a page, or any form that lets the user see the data they need while they decide.

